\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Overall architecture of the proposed attribute-guided H-CAST framework. The input image is processed by the H-CAST backbone to produce hierarchical visual representations for species, family, and order prediction. The proposed attribute-guided module is attached to the species-level branch. Fine graph-node features are used to produce attribute-oriented features, while the species class token is fused with the resulting attribute summary vector before final species classification. Family and order predictions follow the original higher-level H-CAST hierarchy. CUB class-level attributes and the HSDAS mask are used only during training to supervise group-wise attribute predictions. }}{33}{figure.caption.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Detailed structure of the proposed attribute-guided species-level branch. Each active semantic attribute group is represented by a learnable query. The attribute-group queries perform cross-attention over species-level graph-node features to obtain attribute-oriented representations. These representations are supervised by group-wise attribute classifiers during training and are also aggregated into an attribute summary vector through attention pooling. The attribute summary vector is concatenated with the species class token and passed through a fusion MLP before the final species classifier. The dashed region indicates modules added on top of the original H-CAST backbone. }}{51}{figure.caption.4}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Representative intra-family species confusions corrected by the proposed model. In each example, H-CAST predicts an incorrect species while still predicting the correct family, whereas the proposed attribute-guided model predicts the correct species.}}{91}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Species-level attention comparison between the image-only H-CAST rerun and the proposed attribute-guided model. The first example shows a corrected case where the two models produce visibly different attention patterns. The second example shows a corrected case where the attention maps are visually similar, although H-CAST predicts the wrong species and the proposed model predicts the correct species. Warmer regions indicate higher normalized attention scores within the same image. The maps are diagnostic visualizations and should not be interpreted as localization results or direct causal explanations of the prediction changes. }}{95}{figure.caption.18}%
